  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Showers, and potentially a few thunderstorms, \nlikely late this weekend into early next week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nA cold front approaches our area tonight as an area of low \npressure moves north along it Sunday night. The low should pass \nnorth of our area Sunday night into Monday while strengthening. \nModels have come into better agreement on timing, with Monday \nlooking like the period for the heaviest rainfall but there are \nstill some differences in the guidance that prevent higher \nconfidence in this event. Ensembles show about a 60-80% chance \nof at least 0.5 inches of rainfall for most areas, with about a \n30-50% chance of at least 0.5 inches in the Central Highlands \nand Southern and Central Aroostook. Models and ensembles still \ndiffer on where stronger rain bands set up, either over the \nNorth Woods and/or Downeast, with a few showing them set up over\ncentral areas, which could shift totals. An uncertainty with \nthe models is if low pressure can develop over the Gulf of \nMaine. Models have been increasingly showing development of this\nlow with offshore rain bands, increasing totals for the \nDowneast. Some thunderstorms remain possible Sunday over the \nNorth with the advancing cold front and during the day on Monday\nareawide. Currently thinking the greatest chance for storms \nwill be Sunday, especially if clouds are not too widespread. \nMonday will likely feature isolated elevated convection, mostly \naway from the coast. Another key factor to watch with this event\nis fog. Areas of fog, possibly dense, expected tonight and \nSunday night as light southerly winds bring a moist, stable \nmarine layer inland. Expect the densest fog south of the \nKatahdin region. Patchy fog may persist into each morning. \nAnother round of fog is possible with the rain moving out Monday\nnight.",
